\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2.2}{\ignorespaces The same grid, two representations. The $4\times 4$ grid on the left is split (dashed) into four $2\times 2$ quadrants; three are uniform (leaf $0$, leaf $0$, leaf $1$) and the bottom-right still mixes $0$/$1$, so it recurses one more level. The quad tree on the right is the \emph  {reconstruction}: a node is a leaf exactly when its square is uniform, internal otherwise -- LC 427.}}{374}{figure.caption.26}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c25-quad}{{6.2.2}{374}{The same grid, two representations. The $4\times 4$ grid on the left is split (dashed) into four $2\times 2$ quadrants; three are uniform (leaf $0$, leaf $0$, leaf $1$) and the bottom-right still mixes $0$/$1$, so it recurses one more level. The quad tree on the right is the \emph {reconstruction}: a node is a leaf exactly when its square is uniform, internal otherwise -- LC 427}{figure.caption.26}{}}
